<!-- Website teaching layer for kotlin/language-core/value-classes.md. -->

<!-- @opening -->
<p class="story-lead"><code>cancelOrder(userId, orderId)</code> compiles, passes review, and ships. The function's parameters were declared the other way round — <code>(orderId: String, userId: String)</code> — so production tries to cancel an order whose id is a user's id. Both are <code>String</code>, so nothing complained.</p>

Value classes let you give `UserId` and `OrderId` different types, so that swap fails to compile, while the compiler still passes a plain `String` at run time wherever it can. "Wherever it can" is the interesting part: this short chapter shows when the wrapper truly disappears, when it quietly comes back as an object, and why Java callers can find such functions hard to call.

<!-- @part 1. Value Class vs Type Alias vs Data Class :: BEGINNING -->
<!-- @part 2. Boxing: the Rule That Decides Whether You Got the Performance :: MECHANISM -->
<!-- @part 5. Review Checklist :: STAFF -->

<!-- @after 1. Value Class vs Type Alias vs Data Class :: visual -->
### Two worlds: the type checker and the JVM

<figure class="viz"><div class="viz-pair"><div class="pane"><span class="pane-title">What the compiler checks</span><pre>fun cancelOrder(order: <mark>OrderId</mark>, user: <mark>UserId</mark>)

cancelOrder(userId, orderId)
// ✕ type mismatch: UserId is not OrderId</pre></div><div class="pane"><span class="pane-title">What the JVM usually receives</span><pre>cancelOrder-&lt;hash&gt;(String order, String user)

// the wrappers are gone: two plain Strings
// (the name is mangled — see section 3)</pre></div></div><figcaption>The safety lives entirely at compile time. A <code>typealias</code> would give neither: <code>typealias UserId = String</code> accepts any <code>String</code>, including an order id.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after 2. Boxing: the Rule That Decides Whether You Got the Performance :: animation -->
### Follow one `Meters` value through a program

<figure class="stepper"><span class="stepper-title">When does the wrapper become a real object?</span><div class="stage viz-grid" style="--cols:2"><div class="node" data-el="self"><strong><code>asItself(m: Meters)</code></strong><small>used as Meters → passed as a plain <code>double</code></small></div><div class="node" data-el="nullable"><strong><code>asNullable(m: Meters?)</code></strong><small>needs a reference that can be null → boxed</small></div><div class="node" data-el="generic"><strong><code>listOf(m)</code></strong><small>stored as a generic <code>T</code> → boxed representation; allocation depends on the boundary</small></div><div class="node" data-el="reuse"><strong>Two slots can share one box</strong><small><code>fun &lt;T&gt; twice(value: T) = listOf(value, value)</code><br>Inside this helper, both slots keep the same already-boxed reference.</small></div><div class="node" data-el="iface"><strong>used as <code>Any</code> or an interface</strong><small>a more general type → boxed</small></div><div class="node" data-el="back"><strong><code>list[0].v</code></strong><small>read back as Meters → unboxed again</small></div><div class="node" data-el="identity"><strong><code>a === b</code> is rejected</strong><small>for two <code>Meters</code> values, use <code>==</code>; reference identity is observable only after boxing as <code>Any</code></small></div></div><script type="application/json" class="steps">[
{"label":"As itself","caption":"While the code treats the value as Meters, the compiler passes the underlying double. No object exists: this is the zero-cost case.","add":["self"],"state":{"self":"done"}},
{"label":"Nullable","caption":"Meters? must be able to hold null, and a double cannot. So a wrapper object is created at this boundary.","add":["nullable"],"state":{"nullable":"warn"}},
{"label":"Generic","caption":"Generic collections store boxed representations rather than a primitive array layout. The number of distinct wrappers depends on where values are boxed and whether references are reused.","add":["generic"],"state":{"generic":"warn"}},
{"label":"Reuse a boxed value","caption":"Calling the generic helper twice(m) boxes m at its generic boundary. Inside the helper, the two list positions reference that same box; two slots do not require two distinct wrappers.","add":["reuse"],"state":{"reuse":"done","generic":""}},
{"label":"Supertype","caption":"Passing it as Any, or through an interface the value class implements, also needs a real object.","add":["iface"],"state":{"iface":"warn"}},
{"label":"Back to itself","caption":"Where the code uses it as Meters again, the compiler unwraps the box. Equality still works across boxed and unboxed forms.","add":["back"],"state":{"back":"done"}},
{"label":"Identity is not available","caption":"Kotlin rejects a === b when both operands have the value-class type Meters. Compare values with ==. After conversion to Any, reference identity can be observed, but it does not define value equality.","add":["identity"],"state":{"identity":"error"}}
]</script><figcaption>The rule from the chapter, as a picture: inlined when used as itself, boxed when used as anything more general.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after 3. Mangling and Java Interop :: visual -->
### Why Java cannot see the function, and the fix

<figure class="viz"><div class="viz-flow"><div class="node"><strong>Kotlin</strong><small><code>fun compute(x: Millis)</code></small></div><div class="arrow"></div><div class="node" data-state="warn"><strong>Bytecode name</strong><small><code>compute-&lt;hash&gt;(long)</code>: the hyphen keeps it apart from <code>compute(long)</code>, and Java cannot write that name</small></div><div class="arrow"></div><div class="node is-key"><strong><code>@JvmName("computeMillis")</code></strong><small>chooses a Java-legal name: <code>computeMillis(long)</code></small></div></div><figcaption>Decide this before publishing: changing a parameter between a value class and a regular class later changes the method's signature for every compiled caller.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after 4. Where They Earn Their Keep :: check -->
**Check yourself.**

<details><summary>A repository method takes <code>(userId: String, accountId: String, deviceId: String)</code>. Worth value classes?</summary>

Yes — three same-typed identifiers side by side is exactly the transposition risk value classes remove, at no cost in straight-line Kotlin code.
</details>

<details><summary>A hot path keeps millions of <code>Millis</code> values in a <code>List&lt;Millis&gt;</code>. What is the cost?</summary>

The list stores boxed representations. Its length does not determine the number of fresh allocations: references can be reused, as the animation shows. Measure the actual boundary, and consider a `LongArray` for dense primitive storage.
</details>

<details><summary>You need <code>Money(amount, currency)</code>. Value class?</summary>

No. A value class wraps exactly one underlying value; two fields make it a data class.
</details>
